    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear focal point in **Mickey Moniak**, who boasts our highest efficiency score of 75.97. The combination of a favorable matchup against Sasaki and a massive +14% park factor makes **Mickey Moniak** a must-start at the top of the order. Similarly, **Isaac Paredes** earns a strong 64.30 rating as he exploits a favorable platoon advantage against Ure\u00f1a, while **Josh Naylor** (60.48) remains a reliable anchor in the utility spot, benefiting from a neutral-to-positive park environment and a solid middle-of-the-order opportunity against May. \n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today involved **Austin Riley**, who scored a 54.14 but ultimately lacked the efficiency floor to displace the current starters. While **Austin Riley** offers value, his projected spot in the 8-hole and a challenging matchup against Bradley prevented his inclusion. We are exercising caution with several players today; the sub-50 scores for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Griffin Conine** are driven by difficult SP_xERA matchups\u2014specifically Tolle and Nola\u2014and while we are holding these players for their long-term value, their ceiling is capped against these elite arms.\n\nManagers should monitor the status of **Brice Turang**, who faces a stern test against Logan Gilbert (3.41 xERA). Despite the difficult matchup, **Brice Turang** maintains a 53.55 efficiency score, bolstered by his role as a leadoff hitter. Meanwhile, the speed-centric profiles of **Corbin Carroll** and **Max Muncy** are under pressure today; with both facing pitchers who effectively suppress stolen base attempts, their efficiency scores are hampered by the \"Elite Hold SP\" defensive adjustment. We are playing for volume, but with **Trevor Story** remaining sidelined, our bench depth is being tested to ensure we protect our game caps against poor efficiency matchups like the one faced by **Zach McKinstry** against Skenes.",
